### What is postfix evaluation?

Postfix notation, also called Reverse Polish Notation (RPN), places operators after their operands. The expression `3 4 + 2 *` means add 3 and 4 first, then multiply by 2, giving 14. Because the operator order in the string already encodes the evaluation order, there are no parentheses and no precedence rules.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class PostfixEvaluator {
    public static int evaluate(String[] tokens) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (String token : tokens) {
            switch (token) {
                case "+": { int b = stack.pop(); int a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a + b); break; }
                case "-": { int b = stack.pop(); int a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a - b); break; }
                case "*": { int b = stack.pop(); int a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a * b); break; }
                case "/": { int b = stack.pop(); int a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a / b); break; }
                default: stack.push(Integer.parseInt(token));
            }
        }
        return stack.pop();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // (3 + 4) * 2 = 14
        String[] expr = {"3", "4", "+", "2", "*"};
        System.out.println(evaluate(expr)); // 14
    }
}
```

```text
14
```

### Why does the stack make this work?

Each operator needs exactly two operands. The stack holds operands waiting for their operator. When an operator arrives, the two most recent operands are on top of the stack. Popping them, computing, and pushing the result makes that result available as an operand for the next operator. The nesting is handled automatically.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class StackTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] tokens = {"100000", "0.02", "*", "500", "+"};
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (String t : tokens) {
            if (t.matches("-?[0-9.]+")) {
                stack.push(t);
                System.out.println("Push " + t + " -> " + stack);
            } else {
                String b = stack.pop();
                String a = stack.pop();
                double result = switch (t) {
                    case "*" -> Double.parseDouble(a) * Double.parseDouble(b);
                    case "+" -> Double.parseDouble(a) + Double.parseDouble(b);
                    default -> 0;
                };
                stack.push(String.valueOf(result));
                System.out.println(a + " " + t + " " + b + " = " + result + " -> " + stack);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Push 100000 -> [100000]
Push 0.02 -> [0.02, 100000]
100000 * 0.02 = 2000.0 -> [2000.0]
Push 500 -> [500, 2000.0]
2000.0 + 500 = 2500.0 -> [2500.0]
```

### Why does operand order matter?

The first pop is the right operand (b) because it was pushed most recently. The second pop is the left operand (a). For commutative operators like + and * the order does not matter, but for subtraction and division it does. Computing `a - b` versus `b - a` gives different results, and swapping them silently produces wrong answers.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class OperandOrder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Expression: 10 3 - means 10 - 3 = 7, not 3 - 10 = -7
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(10); // a
        stack.push(3);  // b

        int b = stack.pop(); // 3 (right operand)
        int a = stack.pop(); // 10 (left operand)

        System.out.println("Correct: a - b = " + (a - b)); // 7
        System.out.println("Wrong:   b - a = " + (b - a)); // -7
    }
}
```

```text
Correct: a - b = 7
Wrong:   b - a = -7
```

### How do you handle banking amounts with BigDecimal?

For money, use `BigDecimal` and its arithmetic methods. Division must supply an explicit scale and `RoundingMode` to avoid a non-terminating decimal exception when the result has infinite decimal places, like 1 divided by 3.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class PostfixBigDecimal {
    public static BigDecimal evaluate(String[] tokens) {
        Deque<BigDecimal> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (String token : tokens) {
            switch (token) {
                case "+": { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); BigDecimal a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a.add(b)); break; }
                case "-": { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); BigDecimal a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a.subtract(b)); break; }
                case "*": { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); BigDecimal a = stack.pop(); stack.push(a.multiply(b)); break; }
                case "/": { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); BigDecimal a = stack.pop();
                    stack.push(a.divide(b, 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP)); break; }
                default: stack.push(new BigDecimal(token));
            }
        }
        return stack.pop();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 100000 * 0.02 + 500 = 2500.00
        String[] feeFormula = {"100000", "0.02", "*", "500", "+"};
        System.out.println("Fee: " + evaluate(feeFormula));

        // 1000 / 3 = 333.33 (rounded)
        String[] divFormula = {"1000", "3", "/"};
        System.out.println("Division: " + evaluate(divFormula));
    }
}
```

```text
Fee: 2500.00
Division: 333.33
```

### What happens with an invalid expression?

A well-formed postfix expression leaves exactly one value on the stack. If more than one value remains, there are extra operands. If the stack has fewer than two operands when an operator arrives, the expression is malformed. Both cases should be detected and rejected.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class PostfixValidation {
    public static String evaluateWithValidation(String[] tokens) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (String token : tokens) {
            if (token.matches("-?\\d+")) {
                stack.push(Integer.parseInt(token));
            } else {
                if (stack.size() < 2) return "Error: not enough operands for " + token;
                int b = stack.pop();
                int a = stack.pop();
                switch (token) {
                    case "+" -> stack.push(a + b);
                    case "-" -> stack.push(a - b);
                    case "*" -> stack.push(a * b);
                    case "/" -> {
                        if (b == 0) return "Error: division by zero";
                        stack.push(a / b);
                    }
                    default -> { return "Error: unknown operator " + token; }
                }
            }
        }
        if (stack.size() != 1) return "Error: " + stack.size() + " values remain";
        return "Result: " + stack.pop();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluateWithValidation(new String[]{"3", "4", "+"}));        // Result: 7
        System.out.println(evaluateWithValidation(new String[]{"3", "+"}));             // Error
        System.out.println(evaluateWithValidation(new String[]{"3", "4", "5", "+"}));   // Error: 2 values remain
        System.out.println(evaluateWithValidation(new String[]{"10", "0", "/"}));       // Error: division by zero
    }
}
```

```text
Result: 7
Error: not enough operands for +
Error: 2 values remain
Error: division by zero
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is swapping the operand order for subtraction and division. Another is using `double` for money, which introduces floating-point rounding errors. A third is not handling division by zero, which throws `ArithmeticException` for integer division and produces `Infinity` for doubles.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PostfixMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Mistake 1: wrong operand order
        // "10 3 -" should be 10 - 3 = 7, not 3 - 10 = -7
        System.out.println("Always: first pop = right, second pop = left");

        // Mistake 2: double for money
        double wrong = 0.1 + 0.2;
        System.out.println("double: 0.1 + 0.2 = " + wrong); // 0.30000000000000004

        java.math.BigDecimal correct = new java.math.BigDecimal("0.1")
                .add(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.2"));
        System.out.println("BigDecimal: 0.1 + 0.2 = " + correct); // 0.3
    }
}
```

```text
Always: first pop = right, second pop = left
double: 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004
BigDecimal: 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At National Bank of India, the fee-calculation engine stores pricing formulas in postfix form because they are compact and unambiguous. A formula like `100000 0.02 * 500 +` computes 2% of the principal plus a flat charge. Storing formulas as data lets the pricing team change fee logic without redeploying code, and the stack evaluator guarantees exact, correctly rounded amounts using BigDecimal.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class FeeEngine {
    public static BigDecimal computeFee(String[] formula) {
        Deque<BigDecimal> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (String token : formula) {
            switch (token) {
                case "+" -> { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); stack.push(stack.pop().add(b)); }
                case "-" -> { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); stack.push(stack.pop().subtract(b)); }
                case "*" -> { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); stack.push(stack.pop().multiply(b)); }
                case "/" -> { BigDecimal b = stack.pop(); stack.push(stack.pop().divide(b, 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP)); }
                default -> stack.push(new BigDecimal(token));
            }
        }
        return stack.pop();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 2% of 100000 + flat 500 = 2500.00
        String[] formula = {"100000", "0.02", "*", "500", "+"};
        System.out.println("Processing fee: " + computeFee(formula));

        // (principal * rate - discount) = (500000 * 0.015 - 1000) = 6500.00
        String[] formula2 = {"500000", "0.015", "*", "1000", "-"};
        System.out.println("Loan processing fee: " + computeFee(formula2));
    }
}
```

```text
Processing fee: 2500.00
Loan processing fee: 6500.000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Postfix evaluation is one direction of the Shunting-Yard algorithm. The full pipeline is: parse infix to postfix using the Shunting-Yard algorithm, then evaluate postfix with the stack. The Shunting-Yard handles precedence and associativity during conversion, so the evaluator itself never needs to know about them. For production fee engines, this two-phase approach keeps the evaluator simple and the precedence logic isolated.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Full pipeline: infix -> Shunting-Yard -> postfix -> stack eval");
        System.out.println("Shunting-Yard handles precedence and associativity");
        System.out.println("Stack evaluator is always O(n) with no precedence logic");
        System.out.println("Production: store formulas as postfix data, evaluate on demand");
    }
}
```

```text
Full pipeline: infix -> Shunting-Yard -> postfix -> stack eval
Shunting-Yard handles precedence and associativity
Stack evaluator is always O(n) with no precedence logic
Production: store formulas as postfix data, evaluate on demand
```
